# A per-task image for MedakaPolish, kept out of the cluster image on purpose.
#
#   anyscale image build -n wdl-medaka-gpu --containerfile tools/Dockerfile.medaka-gpu
#
# Why this is a separate image rather than another entry in tools/manifest.toml.
#
# medaka is the one tool in this pipeline that does not fit the manifest's model. The others
# are a binary or a small source build with no Python dependencies at all, which is what lets
# them coexist in one environment (see manifest.toml's header). medaka is a PyTorch
# application: it pulls torch plus, on GPU, the whole CUDA runtime, and installing it into the
# cluster image costs 1.2 GB even in the CPU-only configuration.
#
# On 2.56.0 it also broke the base: medaka requires `numpy>=2.0`, that base shipped 1.26.4, and
# upgrading numpy underneath it broke `cupy`, which `ray.util.collective` imports. The 2.58.0
# base ships numpy 2.2.6 and cupy-cuda12x 13.6.0, and `medaka==2.2.2` resolves against its full
# freeze without moving a base package (uv pip compile, 2026-09-24; resolved, not installed). So
# on 2.58.0 the case for a separate image is size and the CUDA runtime, not numpy.
#
# Measured on anyscale/ray:2.56.0-py312, for anyone tempted to put it back in the cluster image:
#
#   pip install medaka                     torch + triton + cuda-toolkit + 15 nvidia-* wheels
#   pip install medaka  (torch CPU index)  1.2 GB, numpy 1.26.4 -> 2.5.2, `import cupy` fails
#   pin numpy==2.2.6                       still fails: cupy-cuda12x 13.4.0 declares
#                                          numpy<2.3 but its wheel was built against the
#                                          numpy 1.x C ABI, so the declaration is not the
#                                          constraint that matters
#   a venv inside the cluster image        works, base env untouched, still 1.2 GB
#
# So it lives here instead, and only the run that asks for polishing pays for it. Nine of the
# ten tasks never touch this image.
#
# HOW IT IS SELECTED. MedakaPolish declares `docker: us.gcr.io/broad-dsp-lrma/lr-medaka:0.1.0`,
# which under `--container-runtime ray` is *mapped* rather than pulled:
#
#   [ray]
#   container_runtime = ray
#   task_image_map = {"us.gcr.io/broad-dsp-lrma/lr-medaka:0.1.0": "anyscale/image/wdl-medaka-gpu:1"}
#   task_image_fallback = cluster
#
# task_image_fallback = cluster sends every other task to the cluster image; without it an
# unmapped task fails to dispatch by design. (A `"*"` entry in the map would instead send them
# all to one image URI.) `wdl-on-ray probe-image` checks a built image against the cluster
# before you depend on it. tools/BUILDING.md covers the rest.
#
# THE VERSION CONSTRAINT IS EXACT. A nested image's Ray and Python must match the cluster's,
# Python to the patch level, so this is built from the CUDA variant of the same base the
# cluster image uses: same Ray 2.58.0, same Python 3.12.13, plus a CUDA runtime.
FROM anyscale/ray:2.58.0-py312-cu128

# No --extra-index-url here, unlike the CPU-only install: the default wheels are the CUDA
# ones, which is the entire point of this image. medaka 2.2.2 ships cp312 wheels and declares
# python >=3.10,<3.14, so the "medaka needs Python <3.11" constraint that kept it out of this
# template belongs to the medaka 1.x line and has not applied since 2.1.1.
#
# torch is pinned because medaka asks only for `torch~=2.9`, and from 2.11 PyPI's default torch
# is CUDA 13 (cuda-toolkit 13.0.x, nvidia-cudnn-cu13), a second runtime this cu128 base does
# not match; unpinned, pip picks 2.14.0. 2.10.0 is the newest still on CUDA 12.8
# (nvidia-cuda-runtime-cu12==12.8.90), read from PyPI metadata 2026-09-24. This image has not
# been built with it yet.
RUN pip install --no-cache-dir medaka==2.2.2 torch==2.10.0

# Fail the build rather than the run, the same way the cluster image's Dockerfile does. A
# missing model or a torch that cannot see CUDA is otherwise discovered after the assembly
# it was supposed to polish has already succeeded.
RUN set -eux; \
    medaka --version; \
    python -c "import torch; print('torch', torch.__version__, 'cuda build:', torch.version.cuda)"; \
    python -c "import torch; assert (torch.version.cuda or '').startswith('12.8'), torch.version.cuda"; \
    medaka tools list_models | tr ',' '\n' | grep -q 'r1041_e82_400bps_sup_v4.1.0'

# The reads and the draft are the task's inputs, but the *model* is downloaded on first use by
# default, which on a GPU node in a private subnet is a run that hangs rather than fails.
# Baking the one this template's chemistry needs makes the task hermetic; add others here if
# your reads differ. `medaka tools list_models` prints what this build carries.
RUN medaka tools download_models --models r1041_e82_400bps_sup_v4.1.0
